{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ws5HWVLC6p1vMkkmPHmwSk","type":"tool_result","content":"529\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n530\t\n531\t### 4.1 What a layout is\n532\t\n533\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\n534\tstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\n535\tfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\n536\tphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\n537\tframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\n538\tadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\n539\tcanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\n540\tBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n541\t§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n542\t\n543\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), identity under image collision (R1,\n544\treplacing codex's F2 amendment).** A is the code identity even when\n545\tdistinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums\n546\tand must not select a source segment. The de Bruijn kernels' offset\n547\ttables carry only the image checksum and refuse a second attach of a\n548\tknown checksum, so a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold two segments of one\n549\timage. Until phase 8 removes those kernels, a de Bruijn receiver refuses\n550\ta carried task whose code holds two A of one image before any stream is\n551\tattached (`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image`), and a de Bruijn source\n552\twhose space resolves one image to several A refuses the lift\n553\t(`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`); no enumeration order selects\n554\tan A. The semantic-register kernel keys code by A and holds\n555\talpha-equivalent segments side by side; this limit is the native\n556\tkernels', not the carrier's. *What the code does:* `layout/space`\n557\tindexes segments by A and, as checksums, by `:register-id` and\n558\t`:stack-id`; `image-segment` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n559\t:ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident}` when an identity names more than\n560\tone A and `:unknown-image` when it names none, so a lift that resolves a\n561\tkernel pc, a return pc or a positional closure's body pc through a\n562\tcollided image refuses inside `canonical-task`, before `rows/lift-task`\n563\tserves anything. `lower-defect`'s first clause (`image-collision`)\n564\tderives the receiver's image for every carried vector and refuses\n565\t`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments\n566\t#{A …}}` for the first identity two A share (identities ordered by their\n567\tprinted form), before `rows/lower-task` and before any attach. *Why:*\n568\tcodex F2 showed `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct A and one R\n569\tand one H; editing both kernels' offset tables for a phase-8 deletion\n570\twas not worth a slice. Tested by\n571\t`layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`, including the reference-lane\n572\tsurvivor round trip (laws 1 and 2 over both closures).\n573\t\n574\t**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n575\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n576\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n577\t`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\n578\tthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n579\t`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n580\t`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n581\t`link-format`). **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the public API as\n582\tlanded (engineer deviation 1, codex: acceptable).** The namespace and\n583\tfile are as proposed; the requires are `dao.jing`,\n584\t`yin.vm.debruijn-code`, `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`, the two kernels\n585\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.register`, `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`), `yin.vm`,\n586\t`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.module`, `.analysis`, `.code`, `.derive`,\n587\t`yin.vm.ucf.rows` and `yin.vm.values`; `yin.vm.debruijn` is not\n588\trequired (the chain is read from A's `:closure` owners, not through\n589\t`resolve-name`). The static half is `segment` (one map per A: `:pairs`,\n590\t`:phys`, `:a->h`, `:h->a`, `:layouts`, `:register`, `:stack`, the two\n591\timage ids), `space`, `segment-of`, `register-pairs`, `correspondence`,\n592\t`phys`, `virt`, `live-out`, `register-live`, `stack-layout`, `chain`,\n593\t`env-of`, `frames-of`, `slices`, `offset`, `kernel-kind`. The dynamic\n594\thalf is one dispatching function each way, `lift-state` and\n595\t`lower-state` (contexts from `lift-context` and `lower-context`), with\n596\tthe `act` and `tail` algorithms separate inside; `canonical-value` and\n597\t`positional-value` convert closures and continuations through guest\n598\tcollections. The task level is `canonical-task`, `kernel-task`,\n599\t`attach-space`, `machine-space`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task` and\n600\t`lower-task` (§7). *Why:* one entry per direction keeps the kernel\n601\tdispatch in one place; the algorithms of §4.2-4.3 are unchanged.\n602\t\n603\t### 4.2 The register kernel's layout"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"553b824c-62b3-4f6f-a68d-09230a1c509d","uuid":"248145ac-8739-411c-87ae-23e9c581c61f","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:18:49.502Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-backends/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n\n### 4.1 What a layout is\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\nstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\nfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\nphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\nframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\nadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\ncanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\nBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), identity under image collision (R1,\nreplacing codex's F2 amendment).** A is the code identity even when\ndistinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums\nand must not select a source segment. The de Bruijn kernels' offset\ntables carry only the image checksum and refuse a second attach of a\nknown checksum, so a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold two segments of one\nimage. Until phase 8 removes those kernels, a de Bruijn receiver refuses\na carried task whose code holds two A of one image before any stream is\nattached (`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image`), and a de Bruijn source\nwhose space resolves one image to several A refuses the lift\n(`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`); no enumeration order selects\nan A. The semantic-register kernel keys code by A and holds\nalpha-equivalent segments side by side; this limit is the native\nkernels', not the carrier's. *What the code does:* `layout/space`\nindexes segments by A and, as checksums, by `:register-id` and\n`:stack-id`; `image-segment` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n:ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident}` when an identity names more than\none A and `:unknown-image` when it names none, so a lift that resolves a\nkernel pc, a return pc or a positional closure's body pc through a\ncollided image refuses inside `canonical-task`, before `rows/lift-task`\nserves anything. `lower-defect`'s first clause (`image-collision`)\nderives the receiver's image for every carried vector and refuses\n`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments\n#{A …}}` for the first identity two A share (identities ordered by their\nprinted form), before `rows/lower-task` and before any attach. *Why:*\ncodex F2 showed `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct A and one R\nand one H; editing both kernels' offset tables for a phase-8 deletion\nwas not worth a slice. Tested by\n`layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`, including the reference-lane\nsurvivor round trip (laws 1 and 2 over both closures).\n\n**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\nthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n`link-format`). **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the public API as\nlanded (engineer deviation 1, codex: acceptable).** The namespace and\nfile are as proposed; the requires are `dao.jing`,\n`yin.vm.debruijn-code`, `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`, the two kernels\n(`yin.vm.debruijn.register`, `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`), `yin.vm`,\n`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.module`, `.analysis`, `.code`, `.derive`,\n`yin.vm.ucf.rows` and `yin.vm.values`; `yin.vm.debruijn` is not\nrequired (the chain is read from A's `:closure` owners, not through\n`resolve-name`). The static half is `segment` (one map per A: `:pairs`,\n`:phys`, `:a->h`, `:h->a`, `:layouts`, `:register`, `:stack`, the two\nimage ids), `space`, `segment-of`, `register-pairs`, `correspondence`,\n`phys`, `virt`, `live-out`, `register-live`, `stack-layout`, `chain`,\n`env-of`, `frames-of`, `slices`, `offset`, `kernel-kind`. The dynamic\nhalf is one dispatching function each way, `lift-state` and\n`lower-state` (contexts from `lift-context` and `lower-context`), with\nthe `act` and `tail` algorithms separate inside; `canonical-value` and\n`positional-value` convert closures and continuations through guest\ncollections. The task level is `canonical-task`, `kernel-task`,\n`attach-space`, `machine-space`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task` and\n`lower-task` (§7). *Why:* one entry per direction keeps the kernel\ndispatch in one place; the algorithms of §4.2-4.3 are unchanged.\n\n### 4.2 The register kernel's layout","numLines":75,"startLine":529,"totalLines":2621}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Ws5HWVLC6p1vMkkmPHmwSk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
