{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016eencdZqx7QgAKHK6mR8AW","type":"tool_result","content":"860\tregister kernel needs no change.\n861\t\n862\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the seam has landed; what the code\n863\tdoes for a tail.** 6c-pre merged (`296f75fd`,\n864\t`stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`); the paragraph above is now a reading\n865\tof code. `lower-state` for a canonical `tail` into the stack kernel emits\n866\t`{:pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result :stack (the K\n867\tslices, in order) :frames (get fill :frames []) :continuation …}`, with\n868\t`:store-of` only when `fill` names one; into the register kernel `{:site-pc\n869\t(+ off site) :pc (+ off site 1) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil :resume-mode\n870\t:return-result :frames (get fill :frames [])}` likewise. The lift of a\n871\tstack entry decides `tail?` by `(code/tail-call? (inst seg site))` at the\n872\tA site, the register lift by the entry's `:resume-mode`;\n873\t`layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable` lowers rows 6 and 20 into\n874\tboth kernels with `[]`/nil and with junk `:frames`/`:store-of` and sees\n875\tequal observables (§5.6 item 8, four cases). *Why:* the seam was a\n876\tspecification when this section was written; it is now code, and the\n877\tlayout's tail shapes are stated as emitted.\n878\t\n879\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the retained-FFI response-pair seam\n880\t(codex D2, accepted by R6 as a seam specification; the last sentence is\n881\tR2's).** On completion of a retained FFI request, each de Bruijn kernel\n882\tpreserves the carried response cursor and response stream when\n883\tconstructing the response wait. If both are present, response-wait-entry\n884\tis overridden with cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref :id response-cursor}\n885\tand stream-id response-stream. A partial carried pair is rejected; the\n886\tkernel's composition-owned response pair is used only for entries that\n887\tcarry neither field. Response decoding precedes delivery, including tail\n888\tdelivery. Row 4 must continue on all six ordered engine pairs with the\n889\treceiving composition's call pair distinct from the source's pair. This\n890\tseam is slice 6c-post-D2, a kernel edit of its own, gated by the owner;\n891\tit is not a prerequisite of 6c. When it lands, `unlowerable` is emptied\n892\tand the law-5 counts rise to 366 and 96. *What the code does today:*\n893\tboth kernels' `:request-sent` re-park builds `ffi/response-wait-entry`\n894\tover the composition's own call pair (`register.cljc:508-514`,\n895\t`stack.cljc:564-570`), so a carried row 4 never wakes on a de Bruijn\n896\treceiver (codex probe 3 reproduced it on all four such pairs); the\n897\tharness's `unlowerable` set `#{[4 :stack] [4 :register]}` keeps those\n898\ttransfers out of law 5 (§5.3). The semantic-register kernel's\n899\t`register-restore` already honours the carried pair\n900\t(`semantic_register.cljc:105-111`). *Why:* phase 6 edits no kernel but\n901\t6c-pre's one seam (§8.5); this second seam is cheap but dies at phase 8\n902\twith the kernels, so it is the owner's call (§11, 6c Q2).\n903\t\n904\t### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n905\t\n906\t**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\n907\tphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\n908\tnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n909\t`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\n910\tlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\n911\tone per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n912\t\n913\t**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n914\t`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n915\t\n916\t```\n917\tE = (merge free-env\n918\t           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n919\t           …\n920\t           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n921\t    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n922\t```\n923\t\n924\tThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n925\t(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n926\t(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\n927\titself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\n928\tduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n929\t`engine/bind-params`'s.\n930\t\n931\t**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\n932\tcounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n933\t\n934\t```clojure\n935\t(let [f (fn [] x)]\n936\t  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n937\t```\n938\t\n939\tAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\n940\tearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\n941\tthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\n942\tclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\n943\ttask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\n944\tcarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\n945\tchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n946\t\n947\t```\n948\tfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\n949\tF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n950\t```\n951\t\n952\t`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\n953\tframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\n954\tunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n955\t`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\n956\tnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\n957\tvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\n958\tframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\n959\tharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\n960\tevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\n961\tdesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n962\t`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n963\t\n964\t**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\n965\tentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\n966\tclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n967\t§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\n968\tbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\n969\ttask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n970\t`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n971\t\n972\t### 4.5 What a layout never does\n973\t\n974\t**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\n975\tcontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\n976\tstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n977\t§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\n978\tinvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\n979\tinstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n980\t\n981\t---\n982\t\n983\t## 5. The safepoint harness\n984\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"553b824c-62b3-4f6f-a68d-09230a1c509d","uuid":"96dbda66-7819-4f17-8b60-6fd3d167912c","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:18:52.588Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-backends/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"register kernel needs no change.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the seam has landed; what the code\ndoes for a tail.** 6c-pre merged (`296f75fd`,\n`stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`); the paragraph above is now a reading\nof code. `lower-state` for a canonical `tail` into the stack kernel emits\n`{:pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result :stack (the K\nslices, in order) :frames (get fill :frames []) :continuation …}`, with\n`:store-of` only when `fill` names one; into the register kernel `{:site-pc\n(+ off site) :pc (+ off site 1) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil :resume-mode\n:return-result :frames (get fill :frames [])}` likewise. The lift of a\nstack entry decides `tail?` by `(code/tail-call? (inst seg site))` at the\nA site, the register lift by the entry's `:resume-mode`;\n`layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable` lowers rows 6 and 20 into\nboth kernels with `[]`/nil and with junk `:frames`/`:store-of` and sees\nequal observables (§5.6 item 8, four cases). *Why:* the seam was a\nspecification when this section was written; it is now code, and the\nlayout's tail shapes are stated as emitted.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the retained-FFI response-pair seam\n(codex D2, accepted by R6 as a seam specification; the last sentence is\nR2's).** On completion of a retained FFI request, each de Bruijn kernel\npreserves the carried response cursor and response stream when\nconstructing the response wait. If both are present, response-wait-entry\nis overridden with cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref :id response-cursor}\nand stream-id response-stream. A partial carried pair is rejected; the\nkernel's composition-owned response pair is used only for entries that\ncarry neither field. Response decoding precedes delivery, including tail\ndelivery. Row 4 must continue on all six ordered engine pairs with the\nreceiving composition's call pair distinct from the source's pair. This\nseam is slice 6c-post-D2, a kernel edit of its own, gated by the owner;\nit is not a prerequisite of 6c. When it lands, `unlowerable` is emptied\nand the law-5 counts rise to 366 and 96. *What the code does today:*\nboth kernels' `:request-sent` re-park builds `ffi/response-wait-entry`\nover the composition's own call pair (`register.cljc:508-514`,\n`stack.cljc:564-570`), so a carried row 4 never wakes on a de Bruijn\nreceiver (codex probe 3 reproduced it on all four such pairs); the\nharness's `unlowerable` set `#{[4 :stack] [4 :register]}` keeps those\ntransfers out of law 5 (§5.3). The semantic-register kernel's\n`register-restore` already honours the carried pair\n(`semantic_register.cljc:105-111`). *Why:* phase 6 edits no kernel but\n6c-pre's one seam (§8.5); this second seam is cheap but dies at phase 8\nwith the kernels, so it is the owner's call (§11, 6c Q2).\n\n### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\nphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\nnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\nlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\none per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n\n**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n\n```\nE = (merge free-env\n           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n           …\n           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n```\n\nThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\nitself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\nduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n`engine/bind-params`'s.\n\n**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\ncounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n\n```clojure\n(let [f (fn [] x)]\n  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n```\n\nAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\nearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\nthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\nclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\ntask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\ncarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\nchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n\n```\nfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\nF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n```\n\n`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\nframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\nunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\nnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\nvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\nframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\nharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\nevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\ndesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n\n**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\nentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\nclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\nbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\ntask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n\n### 4.5 What a layout never does\n\n**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\ncontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\nstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\ninvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\ninstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness\n","numLines":125,"startLine":860,"totalLines":2621}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016eencdZqx7QgAKHK6mR8AW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
