{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J8Lop5JQKbbG28DECcFziV","type":"tool_result","content":"1146\t### 5.3 Lanes\n1147\t\n1148\tThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\n1149\tengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\n1150\tand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\n1151\tthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\n1152\tthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n1153\t9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n1154\t\n1155\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the shared fragment (R2; count\n1156\tamendment ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13, §11 6c Q1).** The de\n1157\tBruijn lanes execute the shared fragment of the matrix: rows 1-6, 13-16,\n1158\t18-19, 21-22 and 24 under the four wrappers, and row 20. Rows 7-12 and\n1159\t17 are not built on them: a de Bruijn kernel links a native image while\n1160\tmodule responses carry A, and §4 defines no layout for install children.\n1161\tRow 4 is lifted from a de Bruijn source but not lowered into a de Bruijn\n1162\treceiver until the retained-FFI seam (§4, 6c-post-D2) lands: the\n1163\tkernel's re-park names its own call pair. These lanes are a cross-engine\n1164\tcheck of the canonical frame and are removed with their kernels at phase\n1165\t8; the reference lane runs the whole matrix. *What the code does:* the\n1166\tharness's `*lane*` (`:semantic-register`, `:stack`, `:register`) selects\n1167\tthe kernel `machine` builds; `load-program` on a de Bruijn lane attaches\n1168\t`v`'s derived image beside what the kernel holds and starts it at the\n1169\timage's offset (`kernel-load`), so parked records keep naming their\n1170\trows; `lane-lift` is `rows/lift-task` on the reference lane and\n1171\t`layout/lift-task` over the world's `kernel-space` otherwise;\n1172\t`lane-lower` is `rows/lower-task` or `layout/lower-task` with a fresh\n1173\tscratch machine. `link-rows` is `#{7 8 9 10 11 12 17}`, `lane-cases`\n1174\tfilters `matrix-cases` by it (61 cases), `unlowerable` is `#{[4 :stack]\n1175\t[4 :register]}`. Every world's layout lookups run inside `scoped`: a\n1176\t`binding`-scoped atom `*projected*` records the vectors that world\n1177\tprojected and `kernel-space` reads that set, so an alpha-equivalent A an\n1178\tunrelated fixture projected widens no lookup; `kernel-space` throws when\n1179\tunbound, and only `file-wide` reads the whole file's `code-index`, in\n1180\tthe one fixture that shows the contamination (§8.3 item 14). *Why:*\n1181\tlink rows need the harness to answer a `require` in\n1182\tthe kernel's own format (H or R) while module responses carry A, install\n1183\tchildren are whole kernels the layout would lift and lower recursively,\n1184\tand the retained-FFI re-park is a kernel edit (§4.3); 2-3 days of\n1185\tharness and layout code for kernels deleted at phase 8 was not worth\n1186\tit (R2). The sentence above on `lift-act`/`lift-tail` is superseded by\n1187\t§4.1: the lift is `lift-state`, dispatching on the kernel's kind.\n1188\t\n1189\t### 5.4 Laws\n1190\t\n1191\t1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n1192\t   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n1193\t   `:368-401`).\n1194\t2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n1195\t   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n1196\t   §5.5.\n1197\t3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n1198\t   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n1199\t4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n1200\t   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n1201\t   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n1202\t5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n1203\t   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n1204\t   reference result.\n1205\t6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n1206\t   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n1207\t   verdict is lane-independent.\n1208\t\n1209\t### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n1210\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"553b824c-62b3-4f6f-a68d-09230a1c509d","uuid":"e084e4a3-5509-4173-bad6-e22faac14480","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:18:52.976Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-backends/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 5.3 Lanes\n\nThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\nengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\nand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\nthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\nthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the shared fragment (R2; count\namendment ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13, §11 6c Q1).** The de\nBruijn lanes execute the shared fragment of the matrix: rows 1-6, 13-16,\n18-19, 21-22 and 24 under the four wrappers, and row 20. Rows 7-12 and\n17 are not built on them: a de Bruijn kernel links a native image while\nmodule responses carry A, and §4 defines no layout for install children.\nRow 4 is lifted from a de Bruijn source but not lowered into a de Bruijn\nreceiver until the retained-FFI seam (§4, 6c-post-D2) lands: the\nkernel's re-park names its own call pair. These lanes are a cross-engine\ncheck of the canonical frame and are removed with their kernels at phase\n8; the reference lane runs the whole matrix. *What the code does:* the\nharness's `*lane*` (`:semantic-register`, `:stack`, `:register`) selects\nthe kernel `machine` builds; `load-program` on a de Bruijn lane attaches\n`v`'s derived image beside what the kernel holds and starts it at the\nimage's offset (`kernel-load`), so parked records keep naming their\nrows; `lane-lift` is `rows/lift-task` on the reference lane and\n`layout/lift-task` over the world's `kernel-space` otherwise;\n`lane-lower` is `rows/lower-task` or `layout/lower-task` with a fresh\nscratch machine. `link-rows` is `#{7 8 9 10 11 12 17}`, `lane-cases`\nfilters `matrix-cases` by it (61 cases), `unlowerable` is `#{[4 :stack]\n[4 :register]}`. Every world's layout lookups run inside `scoped`: a\n`binding`-scoped atom `*projected*` records the vectors that world\nprojected and `kernel-space` reads that set, so an alpha-equivalent A an\nunrelated fixture projected widens no lookup; `kernel-space` throws when\nunbound, and only `file-wide` reads the whole file's `code-index`, in\nthe one fixture that shows the contamination (§8.3 item 14). *Why:*\nlink rows need the harness to answer a `require` in\nthe kernel's own format (H or R) while module responses carry A, install\nchildren are whole kernels the layout would lift and lower recursively,\nand the retained-FFI re-park is a kernel edit (§4.3); 2-3 days of\nharness and layout code for kernels deleted at phase 8 was not worth\nit (R2). The sentence above on `lift-act`/`lift-tail` is superseded by\n§4.1: the lift is `lift-state`, dispatching on the kernel's kind.\n\n### 5.4 Laws\n\n1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n   `:368-401`).\n2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n   §5.5.\n3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n   reference result.\n6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n   verdict is lane-independent.\n\n### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n","numLines":65,"startLine":1146,"totalLines":2621}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J8Lop5JQKbbG28DECcFziV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
