{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FG3NQ1389JaesCwSfA1Zfk","type":"tool_result","content":"1871\t### 8.3 Slice 6c: the layouts, the stack seam, the cross-engine lanes\n1872\t\n1873\t**6c-pre, the stack tail-completion seam.** File to change:\n1874\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (§4.3): park-entry builders mark a\n1875\ttail-call park `:resume-mode :return-result`; `stack-restore` delivers\n1876\tsuch an entry by the return transition. Test to create:\n1877\t`test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`: a tail\n1878\teffectful call parked and resumed delivers through `K` (pops the frame,\n1879\ttruncates to its base, pushes the value) and halts with the value when\n1880\t`K` is empty; a tail call in operand position never executes the layout\n1881\tsyntax after it; non-tail parks are unchanged (the old stack tests stay\n1882\tgreen). **Depends on** nothing of 4b or 5.\n1883\t\n1884\t**6c proper.** Files to create: `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`\n1885\t(§4); `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`; the two extra\n1886\tlanes added to the 6b harness file. **Depends on:** 5a, 5b (`derive`),\n1887\t6b, 6c-pre.\n1888\t\n1889\t**Acceptance (6c).**\n1890\t\n1891\t1. `layout-test/register-pairing`: for every `P ∈ C`, `phys` is total on\n1892\t   every body's named virtuals; `virt(p, s)` is single-valued at every\n1893\t   reachable boundary pc (§5.6 item 3).\n1894\t2. `layout-test/register-live-is-live-out`: §5.6 item 3's second clause,\n1895\t   tail boundaries `[]`.\n1896\t3. `layout-test/register-rd-from-a`: on `:worked-example`'s register\n1897\t   lane at the inner call's park, the canonical `rd` is `2` while the\n1898\t   kernel's `dest` is `3`; the K frame of a nested non-tail call in\n1899\t   `:depth-3` has its `rd` from A, not its physical `dest`.\n1900\t4. `layout-test/stack-correspondence`: §5.6 item 4.\n1901\t5. `layout-test/stack-layout-is-the-saved-set`: §5.6 item 5 and the\n1902\t   worked cases: `(f (g x) y)` at the inner call → `[1]`; `(f (if c (g)\n1903\t   2) y)` at `(g)` → `[1]`.\n1904\t6. `layout-test/stack-slices-partition`: §5.6 item 9; and the\n1905\t   multi-frame fixture, the expression `(cat [1] ((fn [x] (cat [2] ((fn\n1906\t   [y] (cat [3] (next-of c))) 2))) 1))` under the harness's standard\n1907\t   binding setup: `with-cat` outermost, then `(let1 s (stream/make 4)\n1908\t   (let1 c (cursor-of s) …))` around it (the stream is made inside the\n1909\t   program, as every row does: that is this fixture's chosen setup. A\n1910\t   primitive `cat` supplied in the free environment would sit in a live\n1911\t   slot; a primitive is an admitted carrier value (`:prim`) that the\n1912\t   register kernel cannot restore from its saved-register payload, so\n1913\t   the register lane would refuse it `:yin.k/unsupported\n1914\t   :continuation-registers` before attach (**Amended after 6c\n1915\t   (2026-10-13)**, codex D4 by R6: the earlier phrase \"is no carrier\n1916\t   value\" misplaced the limit on the carrier; §4.2, §7.2),\n1917\t   `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:196`; an authentic cursor reference is\n1918\t   machine data with an explicit carrier marker, `engine.cljc:899-905`, so\n1919\t   in-program construction of `c` is a fixture choice, not a carrier\n1920\t   restriction). **Derivation of the expression\n1921\t   alone** (`cat` and `c` taken as given): the outer application of\n1922\t   `(fn [x] …)` pops its closure and `1`, leaving the outer `cat` and\n1923\t   `[1]` pushed, so its frame's base is `2`; the inner application leaves\n1924\t   the middle `cat` and `[2]` as well, base `4`; the current activation\n1925\t   has pushed its `cat` and `[3]` and the read has popped its cursor\n1926\t   operand (`stack.cljc:839`), so the operand stack has six values:\n1927\t   slices `[0,2)` = `[cat [1]]`, `[2,4)` = `[cat [2]]`, current `[4,6)` =\n1928\t   `[cat [3]]`, each slice's `stack-layout` being `[operator-rd\n1929\t   literal-rd]` of its `cat` call and equal to that activation's saved\n1930\t   window. **As run**, the three binding applications each pop their\n1931\t   closure and single operand off an otherwise empty stack, so `K` has\n1932\t   **five** frames with bases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0)\n1933\t   [0,2) [2,4)`, current `[4,6)`, stack length six; the test pins these\n1934\t   bases from the kernel's state, checks the partition, and checks that\n1935\t   lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases (§4.3; the\n1936\t   before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`).\n1937\t7. `layout-test/env-reconstruction`: §5.6 item 6.\n1938\t8. `layout-test/closures-round-trip`: §5.6 item 7.\n1939\t9. `layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable`: §5.6 item 8.\n1940\t10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every case of\n1941\t    the shared fragment under `canonical-under`: 61 cases, each over\n1942\t    exactly three distinct kernel lanes. (**Amended after 6c\n1943\t    (2026-10-13)**, R2, ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13; was \"every\n1944\t    row × segment\", 89. The quick tier runs\n1945\t    `cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare`, the 16 bare cases; the 61\n1946\t    are `^:slow` under `slow/guard`.)\n1947\t11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs over the\n1948\t    shared fragment: 350 transfers (366 once 6c-post-D2 lands, when\n1949\t    `unlowerable` is emptied). (**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**, R2;\n1950\t    was every row × segment × pair, 534. The quick tier runs\n1951\t    `cross-engine-lowering-bare`: 92 transfers, 96 with the seam; row\n1952\t    4's two admitted pairs are asserted by count.)\n1953\t12. `harness/layout-refusals`: `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel\n1954\t    stepped to a non-safepoint pc; a register entry whose `live` was\n1955\t    tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra`\n1956\t    for a unique dead slot, `:window-missing` for a dropped live slot),\n1957\t    or is refused by the layout itself as `:yin.k/non-portable\n1958\t    :register-layout` when the added slot is an ambiguous dead preimage;\n1959\t    the test pins all three. (**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**, R5: the\n1960\t    second clause replaced; an ambiguous tamper cannot honestly be\n1961\t    promised `:window-extra`. The ambiguous fixtures are found over C by\n1962\t    `ambiguous-dead-slots`, a reachable parking pc and a slot R gives two\n1963\t    or more virtuals outside `live-out*` and the destination; the test\n1964\t    asserts at least one exists, and the refusal carries the pc and the\n1965\t    slot. The same deftest pins the four `:continuation-registers`\n1966\t    refusals of §4.2: a primitive in a window, in a lexical binding, in\n1967\t    a closure's capture and nested in a window's guest collection, each\n1968\t    with its path and zero attaches, and each program crossing into the\n1969\t    stack lane with the reference value.)\n1970\t13. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts.\n1971\t\n1972\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the acceptance the fix round added\n1973\t(R1, R3, R4; codex F6; the store-write audit).**\n1974\t\n1975\t14. `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`: `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y]\n1976\t    y)` have distinct `:address` and equal `:register-id` and\n1977\t    `:stack-id`; a de Bruijn source whose space resolves one image to two\n1978\t    A refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image` on both lanes with\n1979\t    zero serves, the file-wide index widens the refusal and the world's\n1980\t    own space lifts; a reference-lane task carrying both closures as\n1981\t    store values refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image` into each\n1982\t    native lane with the receiver's own image identity, both A and zero\n1983\t    attaches; one of the pair carried and the other only projected\n1984\t    lowers with one attach and answers; the reference lane holds both\n1985\t    under laws 1 and 2 (the survivor check).\n1986\t15. `layout-test/free-env-values` and `free-env-nested-values`: §6.3's\n1987\t    refusals both ways with their paths and keys, zero attaches and zero\n1988\t    serves; a primitive and a cursor reference under the same nesting\n1989\t    cross and are used.\n1990\t16. `harness/law-4-controls`: the five controls of §5.5.\n1991\t17. `store-write-audit-test`: the two layout `assoc` sites on the\n1992\t    allowlist with their reason (§7.2); kondo reports no unused require\n1993\t    in `layout_test.cljc` (codex F6).\n1994\t\n1995\tThe `^:slow` matrices and the quick-tier slices follow the test-tier\n1996\tcadence: quick at every checkpoint, slow before big merges and at phase\n1997\tboundaries.\n1998\t\n1999\t### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n2000\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"553b824c-62b3-4f6f-a68d-09230a1c509d","uuid":"431ea247-d45e-43ab-b3ae-bd616d18ba60","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:18:55.594Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-backends/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 8.3 Slice 6c: the layouts, the stack seam, the cross-engine lanes\n\n**6c-pre, the stack tail-completion seam.** File to change:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (§4.3): park-entry builders mark a\ntail-call park `:resume-mode :return-result`; `stack-restore` delivers\nsuch an entry by the return transition. Test to create:\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`: a tail\neffectful call parked and resumed delivers through `K` (pops the frame,\ntruncates to its base, pushes the value) and halts with the value when\n`K` is empty; a tail call in operand position never executes the layout\nsyntax after it; non-tail parks are unchanged (the old stack tests stay\ngreen). **Depends on** nothing of 4b or 5.\n\n**6c proper.** Files to create: `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`\n(§4); `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`; the two extra\nlanes added to the 6b harness file. **Depends on:** 5a, 5b (`derive`),\n6b, 6c-pre.\n\n**Acceptance (6c).**\n\n1. `layout-test/register-pairing`: for every `P ∈ C`, `phys` is total on\n   every body's named virtuals; `virt(p, s)` is single-valued at every\n   reachable boundary pc (§5.6 item 3).\n2. `layout-test/register-live-is-live-out`: §5.6 item 3's second clause,\n   tail boundaries `[]`.\n3. `layout-test/register-rd-from-a`: on `:worked-example`'s register\n   lane at the inner call's park, the canonical `rd` is `2` while the\n   kernel's `dest` is `3`; the K frame of a nested non-tail call in\n   `:depth-3` has its `rd` from A, not its physical `dest`.\n4. `layout-test/stack-correspondence`: §5.6 item 4.\n5. `layout-test/stack-layout-is-the-saved-set`: §5.6 item 5 and the\n   worked cases: `(f (g x) y)` at the inner call → `[1]`; `(f (if c (g)\n   2) y)` at `(g)` → `[1]`.\n6. `layout-test/stack-slices-partition`: §5.6 item 9; and the\n   multi-frame fixture, the expression `(cat [1] ((fn [x] (cat [2] ((fn\n   [y] (cat [3] (next-of c))) 2))) 1))` under the harness's standard\n   binding setup: `with-cat` outermost, then `(let1 s (stream/make 4)\n   (let1 c (cursor-of s) …))` around it (the stream is made inside the\n   program, as every row does: that is this fixture's chosen setup. A\n   primitive `cat` supplied in the free environment would sit in a live\n   slot; a primitive is an admitted carrier value (`:prim`) that the\n   register kernel cannot restore from its saved-register payload, so\n   the register lane would refuse it `:yin.k/unsupported\n   :continuation-registers` before attach (**Amended after 6c\n   (2026-10-13)**, codex D4 by R6: the earlier phrase \"is no carrier\n   value\" misplaced the limit on the carrier; §4.2, §7.2),\n   `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:196`; an authentic cursor reference is\n   machine data with an explicit carrier marker, `engine.cljc:899-905`, so\n   in-program construction of `c` is a fixture choice, not a carrier\n   restriction). **Derivation of the expression\n   alone** (`cat` and `c` taken as given): the outer application of\n   `(fn [x] …)` pops its closure and `1`, leaving the outer `cat` and\n   `[1]` pushed, so its frame's base is `2`; the inner application leaves\n   the middle `cat` and `[2]` as well, base `4`; the current activation\n   has pushed its `cat` and `[3]` and the read has popped its cursor\n   operand (`stack.cljc:839`), so the operand stack has six values:\n   slices `[0,2)` = `[cat [1]]`, `[2,4)` = `[cat [2]]`, current `[4,6)` =\n   `[cat [3]]`, each slice's `stack-layout` being `[operator-rd\n   literal-rd]` of its `cat` call and equal to that activation's saved\n   window. **As run**, the three binding applications each pop their\n   closure and single operand off an otherwise empty stack, so `K` has\n   **five** frames with bases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0)\n   [0,2) [2,4)`, current `[4,6)`, stack length six; the test pins these\n   bases from the kernel's state, checks the partition, and checks that\n   lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases (§4.3; the\n   before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`).\n7. `layout-test/env-reconstruction`: §5.6 item 6.\n8. `layout-test/closures-round-trip`: §5.6 item 7.\n9. `layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable`: §5.6 item 8.\n10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every case of\n    the shared fragment under `canonical-under`: 61 cases, each over\n    exactly three distinct kernel lanes. (**Amended after 6c\n    (2026-10-13)**, R2, ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13; was \"every\n    row × segment\", 89. The quick tier runs\n    `cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare`, the 16 bare cases; the 61\n    are `^:slow` under `slow/guard`.)\n11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs over the\n    shared fragment: 350 transfers (366 once 6c-post-D2 lands, when\n    `unlowerable` is emptied). (**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**, R2;\n    was every row × segment × pair, 534. The quick tier runs\n    `cross-engine-lowering-bare`: 92 transfers, 96 with the seam; row\n    4's two admitted pairs are asserted by count.)\n12. `harness/layout-refusals`: `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel\n    stepped to a non-safepoint pc; a register entry whose `live` was\n    tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra`\n    for a unique dead slot, `:window-missing` for a dropped live slot),\n    or is refused by the layout itself as `:yin.k/non-portable\n    :register-layout` when the added slot is an ambiguous dead preimage;\n    the test pins all three. (**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**, R5: the\n    second clause replaced; an ambiguous tamper cannot honestly be\n    promised `:window-extra`. The ambiguous fixtures are found over C by\n    `ambiguous-dead-slots`, a reachable parking pc and a slot R gives two\n    or more virtuals outside `live-out*` and the destination; the test\n    asserts at least one exists, and the refusal carries the pc and the\n    slot. The same deftest pins the four `:continuation-registers`\n    refusals of §4.2: a primitive in a window, in a lexical binding, in\n    a closure's capture and nested in a window's guest collection, each\n    with its path and zero attaches, and each program crossing into the\n    stack lane with the reference value.)\n13. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the acceptance the fix round added\n(R1, R3, R4; codex F6; the store-write audit).**\n\n14. `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`: `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y]\n    y)` have distinct `:address` and equal `:register-id` and\n    `:stack-id`; a de Bruijn source whose space resolves one image to two\n    A refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image` on both lanes with\n    zero serves, the file-wide index widens the refusal and the world's\n    own space lifts; a reference-lane task carrying both closures as\n    store values refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image` into each\n    native lane with the receiver's own image identity, both A and zero\n    attaches; one of the pair carried and the other only projected\n    lowers with one attach and answers; the reference lane holds both\n    under laws 1 and 2 (the survivor check).\n15. `layout-test/free-env-values` and `free-env-nested-values`: §6.3's\n    refusals both ways with their paths and keys, zero attaches and zero\n    serves; a primitive and a cursor reference under the same nesting\n    cross and are used.\n16. `harness/law-4-controls`: the five controls of §5.5.\n17. `store-write-audit-test`: the two layout `assoc` sites on the\n    allowlist with their reason (§7.2); kondo reports no unused require\n    in `layout_test.cljc` (codex F6).\n\nThe `^:slow` matrices and the quick-tier slices follow the test-tier\ncadence: quick at every checkpoint, slow before big merges and at phase\nboundaries.\n\n### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n","numLines":130,"startLine":1871,"totalLines":2621}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FG3NQ1389JaesCwSfA1Zfk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
