{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DxALyazhcRLALSHzk4H4Yo","type":"tool_result","content":"2034\t## 9. Verification honesty\n2035\t\n2036\t- **Hand-derived numbers.** `saved(4, 2) = {1}` and `L(5)` on the worked\n2037\t  example are the phase-3 tests' (`analysis_test.cljc:131-139`). The\n2038\t  stack slices `[f]` at the inner call of `(f (g x) y)` and at `(g)` in\n2039\t  `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` were derived twice from the §4.3 walk and agreed\n2040\t  with the reviewer's derivation; the register `phys(1) = 0`, `phys(2) =\n2041\t  3` follow from phase-5 §6.3 row 2, whose pinned `live` at pc 3 is `[0]`\n2042\t  (`{T0,T3}` minus the destination `T3`), the same set §4.2 step 4 and\n2043\t  §5.6 item 3 compute by subtracting the virtual `rd 2` before mapping.\n2044\t  The multi-frame bases in 6c item 6 (`2, 4` for the expression alone;\n2045\t  `0, 0, 0, 2, 4` as run under the harness's bindings) were derived\n2046\t  twice from the kernel's call arm (`stack.cljc:716-733`, base = height\n2047\t  after popping callee and operands); the first pass forgot the pushed\n2048\t  operator, and revision 2's lowering rule used the height **before**\n2049\t  each slice, which the round-2 review caught and §4.3 now states as\n2050\t  cumulative through the slice. Nothing was run.\n2051\t- **Claims that are checks, not theorems**: the stack operand slice\n2052\t  equals the saved set at non-tail safepoints (§5.6 item 5); the non-push\n2053\t  H ↔ A correspondence (item 4); `virt` single-valued at reachable\n2054\t  boundaries (item 3); layout `E` = evaluator `E` (item 6); the tail\n2055\t  lowering's unused fields are unobservable (item 8); the slices\n2056\t  partition the stack (item 9).\n2057\t- **What is not claimed**: that kernel state round-trips (shadowed outer\n2058\t  positional values are nil-filled; the law is on canonical frames);\n2059\t  that every kernel reconstructs canonical frames (two are named; the\n2060\t  walker is excluded); that today's stack kernel delivers a tail effect\n2061\t  through `K` (it does not; 6c-pre adds it).\n2062\t- **Private access.** Every function a test names is public; no `#'`.\n2063\t- **Unverified.** 4b stage 2 is not landed: rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 are\n2064\t  written against the evaluator design's §1.4-1.6 shapes and the stage-1\n2065\t  branch and must be re-read when stage 2 lands. The seam of §4.3 is a\n2066\t  specification of a change to `stack.cljc`, not a reading of code that\n2067\t  exists. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** 4b stage 2 landed before\n2068\t  6b, and rows 3-4, 7-12, 17 and 24 now run on the reference lane (89\n2069\t  labelled row × segment executions, 85 distinct builder/wrapper setups\n2070\t  since rows 3 and 24 share one builder by design); rows 10-11 were\n2071\t  re-read and corrected (§5.2, B1). The 6b harness ran on the JVM only;\n2072\t  Node and Dart execution of `rows.cljc`'s transport and the new harness\n2073\t  file remain the three-host gate of §8.2 item 9, not established by the\n2074\t  reviews.\n2075\t- **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13).** 6c-pre landed (`296f75fd`) and the\n2076\t  stack kernel delivers a tail effect through `K`; §4.3's seam is now a\n2077\t  reading of code. The layouts' counts are the tests' own assertions,\n2078\t  run on the JVM by the engineer and by codex (quick: 60 tests, 3,615\n2079\t  assertions; slow: 4 tests, 2,419 assertions, before the fix round):\n2080\t  law 4 on 16 bare and 61 full cases over three distinct lanes, law 5 on\n2081\t  92 and 350 transfers, 128 `E` comparisons, four tail cases, more than\n2082\t  twenty slice partitions and more than fifty reachable boundaries. The\n2083\t  rulings' claims that are checks, not theorems: that C holds an\n2084\t  ambiguous dead preimage (asserted, §8.3 item 12); that the alpha pair\n2085\t  derives one R and one H (asserted, item 14). **What this document has\n2086\t  no record of:** a Node or Dart run of `layout.cljc` and the 6c tests.\n2087\t  Codex read the pinned ClojureDart source and found `satisfies?`,\n2088\t  `memoize` and `binding` implemented; that is a source reading, not a\n2089\t  run. The slice merged (`781a9ba8`) and §8.3 item 13 names the gate;\n2090\t  the sources this revision read (the engineer report, the codex review,\n2091\t  the rulings, the merged diff) contain no three-host result, so this\n2092\t  document does not claim one.\n2093\t- **Limits stated as such, with their shelf life.** Image collision\n2094\t  (§4.1), free-environment procedures on the native lanes (§6.3),\n2095\t  primitives in the register kernel's payload (§4.2), the unique\n2096\t  dead-preimage fallback of `virt` (§4.2) and the shared fragment (§5.3)\n2097\t  are limits of the de Bruijn kernels and are removed with them at phase\n2098\t  8; none is a limit of the carrier or of the semantic-register kernel,\n2099\t  and each is pinned by a refusal test rather than left as an\n2100\t  unobserved gap.\n2101\t\n2102\t---\n2103\t\n2104\t## 10. Findings and proposed amendments\n2105\t\n2106\tReviewer rulings (codex gpt-6.1-sol, 2026-10-10): F1, F4, U2, U4, U5,\n2107\tR1, R2, R7, C1 **ACCEPT**; F2, F3, U1, U3, U6, R3, R4, R5, R6, R8, R9\n2108\t**ACCEPT WITH CHANGES**, applied below; dropping `:yin.k/engine`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"553b824c-62b3-4f6f-a68d-09230a1c509d","uuid":"a002452c-2ca4-4027-8e22-c4a19f5e20c9","timestamp":"2026-10-13T10:18:56.378Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-backends/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"## 9. Verification honesty\n\n- **Hand-derived numbers.** `saved(4, 2) = {1}` and `L(5)` on the worked\n  example are the phase-3 tests' (`analysis_test.cljc:131-139`). The\n  stack slices `[f]` at the inner call of `(f (g x) y)` and at `(g)` in\n  `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` were derived twice from the §4.3 walk and agreed\n  with the reviewer's derivation; the register `phys(1) = 0`, `phys(2) =\n  3` follow from phase-5 §6.3 row 2, whose pinned `live` at pc 3 is `[0]`\n  (`{T0,T3}` minus the destination `T3`), the same set §4.2 step 4 and\n  §5.6 item 3 compute by subtracting the virtual `rd 2` before mapping.\n  The multi-frame bases in 6c item 6 (`2, 4` for the expression alone;\n  `0, 0, 0, 2, 4` as run under the harness's bindings) were derived\n  twice from the kernel's call arm (`stack.cljc:716-733`, base = height\n  after popping callee and operands); the first pass forgot the pushed\n  operator, and revision 2's lowering rule used the height **before**\n  each slice, which the round-2 review caught and §4.3 now states as\n  cumulative through the slice. Nothing was run.\n- **Claims that are checks, not theorems**: the stack operand slice\n  equals the saved set at non-tail safepoints (§5.6 item 5); the non-push\n  H ↔ A correspondence (item 4); `virt` single-valued at reachable\n  boundaries (item 3); layout `E` = evaluator `E` (item 6); the tail\n  lowering's unused fields are unobservable (item 8); the slices\n  partition the stack (item 9).\n- **What is not claimed**: that kernel state round-trips (shadowed outer\n  positional values are nil-filled; the law is on canonical frames);\n  that every kernel reconstructs canonical frames (two are named; the\n  walker is excluded); that today's stack kernel delivers a tail effect\n  through `K` (it does not; 6c-pre adds it).\n- **Private access.** Every function a test names is public; no `#'`.\n- **Unverified.** 4b stage 2 is not landed: rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 are\n  written against the evaluator design's §1.4-1.6 shapes and the stage-1\n  branch and must be re-read when stage 2 lands. The seam of §4.3 is a\n  specification of a change to `stack.cljc`, not a reading of code that\n  exists. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** 4b stage 2 landed before\n  6b, and rows 3-4, 7-12, 17 and 24 now run on the reference lane (89\n  labelled row × segment executions, 85 distinct builder/wrapper setups\n 